  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A plume of tropical moisture characterized by \n2\"+ PWATs and dewpoints in the 70s is forecast to continue to \noverspread the Carolinas to the east of the northward-advancing \nremnants of TC Isaias this morning. At the surface, a warm \nfront/coastal trough (currently just south of the area) will \ntrudge north along the southeastern coastline, likely with a \nweak, non- tropical area of low pressure developing along it.\nNow it appears the main axis of convection will remain over the\nGulf Stream, with heavy storms offshore robbing some of the \nrich moisture advection into ENC. This has been a notable trend \nwith most of the CAMs the past several runs. For this reason, \nhave cont to trend down with QPF, especially inland. Most model \nestimates the potential for several inches along the immediate \ncoast, with much lower amts less than an inch or so for the \ninterior Coastal Plain counties. Will continue Flood Watch for \nareas along the coast, effective through through this afternoon,\nthough bulk of widespread rain will be over by mid morning.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Possibility of convective elements reaching the\ncoast would warrant a tornado risk early this morning as the \naforementioned warm front lifts through ENC. Plenty of low level\nhelicity in place with the inc serly flow in place and strong \nveering wind profile downstairs. The greatest tor threat would \nlikely be focused near the srn/sern coast where some better \ninstability advects onshore. Later today, flow becomes more \nunidirectional with swrly low level winds ensuing during the \nafternoon. While helicity wanes, better instability will be in \nplace due to daytime highs expected in the low 80s and TD's \nsurging through the mid 70s. Will have to watch for a severe \nthreat this afternoon ahead of the cold front, with best \nchances appearing for the interior zones, where NCAR/HRRR svr \nprobs are highest. Main concern this afternoon will be potential\nfor iso damaging wind gusts. \n\nThe remnants of Isaias are forecast to round the upper level \nridge and move off the Northern Mid-Atlantic Coast early next \nweek. Drier air then moves in behind it with drying conditions\ndeveloping tonight into Mon.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...Strong onshore winds, building seas, and higher\nthan normal astronomical tides may support some coastal impacts\nthrough early Mon. There remains uncertainty on the magnitude \nof impacts, especially given the shorter-duration of the \nstronger winds, as well as what should be a weaker swell and \nperiodicity compared to the recent Nor'easter. With that in \nmind, there will be an increased risk of rip currents and large \nwaves, at a minimum. High Surf Advisory remains in effect \nbetween Ocracoke and Oregon Inlets. Additionally, some of the \nmost vulnerable locations along the Outer Banks may see at least\nsome minor issues, especially with this morning's high tide \n(7-9am). There may also be some very minor (sub advisory) \ncoastal flooding around sounds and tidal waterways around the \nCrystal Coast due to the higher astronomical tides.",
